stablished restaurants and so we  were going into 2020  as highest we've been in five years  coronavirus quickly changed that  kevin says overall revenue has been down  50  since the pandemic hit the duck inn used  to seat nearly 200 diners between their  indoor and outdoor spaces  now they could host 44 people outdoors  only  and while kevin says delivery and  takeout is up a bit  it still only accounts for five to  fifteen percent of what they make on a  given night  it's been really really rough it's you  know the pandemic hit  we were shut down and we immediately  said okay we gotta find some revenue  so we put all of our wine and all of our  high-end whiskey  online and said you can come and buy all  this they bought it all in april kevin  received a loan from the federal  paycheck protection program  or ppp the loan was intended to provide  relief to small businesses in need  and the eight allowed kevin to cover two  months of payroll  i don't think that we would have been  able to survive without ppp  you know we were cutting everywhere we  could but once the ppp came  okay i can pay their salaries i can pay  health insurance  i can pay the rent i can pay the  utilities this makes a huge difference  that aid helped kevin survive until june  when chicago's reopening plan allowed  for outdoor dining as long as all tables  were at least six feet apart  in early july chicago allowed indoor  dining at 25  capacity no dining area in or out can  have more than 50 people  in late august the state announced new  regulations requiring customers at bars  and restaurants to keep their masks on  while interacting with staff with the  summer coming to an end  very soon we are extremely concerned  about how we're going to maintain  i'm on my third contractor trying to  build  an outdoor coverage but everyone says  they're  too busy we have an architectural  rendering for an outdoor covering that's  really awesome  we just don't have anybody that can  build it operating in c